% closefile on an encoding filter writes the coding's end-of-data marker
% to the data target (PLRM 3.13.1, "applying closefile to an encoding
% filter flushes both the buffered data and the filter's internal state.
% This causes all encoded data to be written to the data target, followed
% by an EOD marker"). The same section says that writing to a filter whose
% data target cannot accept data causes an ioerror, so a target that has
% run out of room must not leave closefile reporting success over a stream
% that stops short of its own terminator.
%
% The target here is a character device that answers every write with "no
% space left". A platform that has no such target cannot pose the question:
% the run then reports INCONCLUSIVE and the harness skips.
%
% Prints SUCCESS iff every encoding filter reports the end-of-data it could
% not write.

% Two layers of buffering stand between a write and the target's refusal,
% and both have to be defeated for the refusal to have happened by the
% time the filter is closed. The coding buffers, so the data has to be
% data it cannot shrink: a block of zeros, or the same block twice, comes
% out of every one of these codings small enough to sit in the target's
% buffer for the whole run. The target buffers too, so enough of it has
% to go in to force the flush that reaches the device.
% The generator is written out here rather than taken from rand, whose
% sequence repeats often enough within one block for every coding to
% shrink the block to a few hundred bytes.
/blk 16384 string def
/seed 1 def
/refill { % -  .  -   the next block of the sequence
    0 1 blk length 1 sub {
        /seed seed 75 mul 74 add 65537 mod def
        blk exch seed 255 and put
    } for
} bind def
/failures 0 def

/pump { % file  .  -
    /pumped exch def
    8 { refill pumped blk writestring } repeat
} bind def

/shut { % file  .  -   close, ignoring whatever it has to say
    { closefile } stopped pop
} bind def

% ---- is there a target here that refuses bytes? ----
/refuses false def
/t0 null def
mark
{
    /t0 (/dev/full) (w) file def
    t0 pump
} stopped {
    $error /errorname get /ioerror eq { /refuses true def } if
} if
t0 null ne { t0 shut } if
cleartomark

refuses not {
    (INCONCLUSIVE: no data target that refuses data on this platform\n) print
    flush
    quit
} if

% ---- every coding with an end-of-data marker reports the one it lost ----
/reports { % /FilterName  .  bool
    /nm exch def
    /rep false def
    mark
    /tgt (/dev/full) (w) file def
    /enc tgt nm filter def
    { enc pump } stopped pop      % the target refuses: that is the premise
    { enc closefile } stopped /rep exch def
    tgt shut
    cleartomark
    rep
} bind def

/available { % /FilterName  .  bool
    /nm2 exch def
    /av false def
    mark
    {
        /probe (/dev/null) (w) file def
        probe nm2 filter shut
        probe shut
    } stopped not /av exch def
    cleartomark
    av
} bind def

/check { % /FilterName  .  -
    dup available {
        dup reports {
            pop
        }{
            (FAIL: closefile hid the end-of-data of ) print ==
            /failures failures 1 add def
        } ifelse
    }{
        pop
    } ifelse
} bind def

/ASCIIHexEncode check
/ASCII85Encode check
/RunLengthEncode check
/LZWEncode check
/CCITTFaxEncode check
/FlateEncode check

% DCTEncode fixes its extent up front, so it takes a parameter dictionary,
% and the image has to be big enough for the compressed form to reach the
% target rather than sit in its buffer
/dctrep false def
/dctran false def
mark
{
    /tgt2 (/dev/full) (w) file def
    /enc2 tgt2 << /Columns 256 /Rows 256 /Colors 1 >> /DCTEncode filter def
    /dctran true def
    { enc2 pump } stopped pop
    { enc2 closefile } stopped /dctrep exch def
    tgt2 shut
} stopped pop
cleartomark
dctran dctrep not and {
    (FAIL: closefile hid the end-of-data of /DCTEncode\n) print
    /failures failures 1 add def
} if

failures 0 eq { (SUCCESS\n) print } if
flush
